l dimensions)\n47:             homology_coeff_field (int): homology field coefficient. Must be a prime number. Default value is 11. Max is 46337.\n48:         \"\"\"\n49:         super().__init__(dynamic=True, **kwargs)\n50:         self.dimensions = homology_dimensions\n51:         self.min_persistence = min_persistence if min_persistence is not None else [0.] * len(self.dimensions)\n52:         self.hcf = homology_coeff_field\n53:         assert len(self.min_persistence) == len(self.dimensions)\n54: \n55:     def call(self, X):\n56:         \"\"\"\n57:         Compute persistence diagram associated to a cubical complex filtered by some pixel values \n58: \n59:         Parameters:\n60:             X (TensorFlow variable): pixel values of the cubical complex\n61: \n62:         Returns:\n63:             List[Tuple[tf.Tensor,tf.Tensor]]: List of cubical persistence diagrams. The length of this list is the same than that of dimensions, i.e., there is one persistence diagram per homology dimension provided in the input list dimensions. Moreover, the finite and essential parts of the persistence diagrams are provided separately: each element of this list is a tuple of size two that contains the finite and essential parts of the corresponding persistence diagram, of shapes [num_finite_points, 2] and [num_essential_points, 1] respectively. Note that the essential part is always empty in cubical persistence diagrams, except in homology dimension zero, where the essential part always contains a single point, with abscissa equal to the smallest value in the complex, and infinite ordinate\n64:         \"\"\"\n65:         # Compute pixels associated to positive and negative simplices \n66:         # Don't compute gradient for this operation\n67:         Xflat = tf.reshape(X, [-1])\n68:         Xdim, Xflat_numpy = X.shape, Xflat.numpy()\n69:         indices_list = _Cubical(Xflat_numpy, Xdim, self.dimensions, self.hcf)\n70:         index_essential = np.argmin(Xflat_numpy) # index