ent numbers of points.\n41:         \"\"\"\n42:         grid_shape = self.grid.shape\n43:         indices = []\n44:         for dim in range(2):\n45:             [m,M] = self.grid_bnds[dim]\n46:             coords = tf.expand_dims(diagrams[:,:,dim],-1)\n47:             ids = grid_shape[dim]*(coords-m)/(M-m)\n48:             indices.append(tf.cast(ids, tf.int32))\n49:         weight = tf.gather_nd(params=self.grid, indices=tf.concat(indices, axis=2))\n50:         return weight\n51:     \n52: class GaussianMixturePerslayWeight(tf.keras.layers.Layer):\n53:     \"\"\"\n54:     This is a class for computing a differentiable weight function for persistence diagram points. This function is defined from a mixture of Gaussian functions.\n55:     \"\"\"\n56:     def __init__(self, gaussians, **kwargs):\n57:         \"\"\"\n58:         Constructor for the GridPerslayWeight class.\n59:   \n60:         Parameters:\n61:             gaussians (4 x n numpy array): parameters of the n Gaussian functions, of the form transpose([[mu_x^1, mu_y^1, sigma_x^1, sigma_y^1], ..., [mu_x^n, mu_y^n, sigma_x^n, sigma_y^n]]). \n62:         \"\"\"\n63:         super().__init__(dynamic=True, **kwargs)\n64:         self.W = tf.Variable(initial_value=gaussians, trainable=True)\n65: \n66:     def build(self, input_shape):\n67:         return self\n68:         \n69:     def call(self, diagrams):\n70:         \"\"\"\n71:         Apply GaussianMixturePerslayWeight on a ragged tensor containing a list of persistence diagrams.\n72: \n73:         Parameters:\n74:             diagrams (n x None x 2): ragged tensor containing n persistence diagrams. The second dimension is ragged since persistence diagrams can have different numbers of points.\n75: \n76:         Returns:\n77:             weight (n x None): ragged tensor containing the weights of the points in the n persistence diagrams. The second dimension is ragged since persistence diagrams can have different numbers of points.\n78:         \"\"\"\n79:         means     = tf.expand_dims(tf.expand_dims(self.W[:2,:],0),0)\n80:         variances = tf.expand_dims(tf.expand_dims(self.W[2:,:],0),0)\n81:         diagrams  = tf.expand_dims(diagrams, -1)\n82:         dists     = tf.math.multiply(tf.math.square(diagrams-means), 1/tf.math.square(variances))\n83:         weight    = tf.math.reduce_sum(tf.math.exp(tf.math.reduce_sum(-dists, axis=2)), axis=2)\n84:         return weight\n85:     \n86: class PowerPerslayWeight(tf.keras.layers.Layer):\n87:     \"\"\"\n88:     This is a class for computing a differentiable weight function for persistence diagram points. This function is defined as a constant multiplied by the distance to the diagonal of the persistence diagram point raised to some power.\n89:     \"\"\"\n90:     def __init__(self, constant, power, **kwargs):\n91:         \"\"\"\n92:         Constructor for the PowerPerslayWeight class.\n93:   \n94:         Parameters:\n95:             constant (float): constant value.\n96:             power (float): power applied to the distance to the diagonal. \n97:         \"\"\"\n98:         super().__init__(dynamic=True, **kwargs)\n99:         self.constant = tf.Variable(initial_value=constant, trainable=True)\n100:         self.power = power\n101:         \n102:     def build(self, input_shape):\n103:         return self\n104:     \n105:     def call(self, diagrams):\n106:         \"\"\"\n107:         Apply PowerPerslayWeight on a ragged tensor containing a list of persistence diagrams.\n108: \n109:         Parameters:\n110:             diagrams (n x None x 2): ragged tensor containing n persistence diagrams. The second dimension is ragged since persistence diagrams can have different numbers of points.\n111: \n112:         Returns:\n113:             weight (n x None): ragged tensor containing the weights of the points in the n persistence diagrams. The second dimension is ragged since persistence diagrams can have different numbers of points.\n114:         \"\"\"\n115:         weight = self.constant * tf.math.pow(tf.math.abs(diagrams[:,:,1]-diagrams[:,:,0]), self.power)\n116:         return weight\n117:     \n118: \n119: class GaussianPerslayPhi(tf.keras.layers.Layer):\n120:     \"\"\"\n121:     This is a class for computing a transformation function for persistence diagram points. This function turns persistence diagram points into 2D Gaussian functions centered on the points, that are then evaluated on a regular 2D grid.\n122:     \"\"\"\n123:     def __init__(self, image_size, image_bnds, variance, **kwargs):\n124:         \"\"\"\n125:         Constructor for the GaussianPerslayPhi class.\n126:   \n127:         Parameters:\n128:             image_size (int numpy array): number of grid elements on each grid axis, of the form [n_x, n_y].\n129:             image_bnds (2 x 2 numpy array): boundaries of the grid, of the form [[min_x, max_x], [min_y, max_y]].\n130:             variance (float): variance of the Gaussian functions. \n131:         \"\"\"\n132:         super().__init__(dynamic=True, **kwargs)\n133:         self.image_size = image_size\n134:         self.image_bnds = image_bnds\n135:         self.variance   = tf.Variable(initial_value=variance, trainable=True)\n136:         \n137:     def build(self, input_shape):\n138:         return self\n139:         \n140:     def call(self, diagrams):\n141:         \"\"\"\n142:         Apply GaussianPerslayPhi on a ragged tensor containing a list of persistence diagrams.\n143: \n144:         Parameters:\n145:             diagrams (n x None x 2): ragged tensor containing n persistence diagrams. The second dimension is ragged since persistence diagrams can have different numbers of points.\n146: \n147:         Returns:\n148:             output (n x None x image_size x image_size x 1): ragged tensor containing the evaluations on the 2D grid of the 2D Gaussian functions corresponding to the persistence diagram points, in the form of a 2D image with 1 channel that can be processed with, e.g., convolutional layers. The second dimension is ragged since persistence diagrams can have different numbers of points.\n149:             output_shape (int numpy array): shape of the output tensor.\n150:         \"\"\"\n151:         diagrams_d = tf.concat([diagrams[:,:,0:1], diagrams[:,:,1:2]-diagrams[:,:,0:1]], axis=2)\n152:         step = [(self.image_bnds[i][1]-self.image_bnds[i][0])/self.image_size[i] for i in range(2)]\n153:         coords = [tf.range(self.image_bnds[i][0], self.image_bnds[i][1], step[i]) for i in range(2)]\n154:         M = tf.meshgrid(*coords)\n155:         mu = tf.concat([tf.expand_dims(tens, 0) for tens in M], axis=0)\n156:         for _ in range(2):\n157:             diagrams_d = tf.expand_dims(diagrams_d,-1)\n158:         dists = tf.math.square(diagrams_d-mu) / (2*tf.math.square(self.variance))\n159:         gauss = tf.math.exp(tf.math.reduce_sum(-dists, axis=2)) / (2*math.pi*tf.math.square(self.variance))\n160:         output = tf.expand_dims(gauss,-1)\n161:         output_shape = M[0].shape + tuple([1])\n162:         return output, output_shape\n163:      \n164: class TentPerslayPhi(tf.keras.layers.Layer):\n165:     \"\"\"\n166:     This is a class for computing a transformation function for persistence diagram points. This function turns persistence diagram points into 1D tent functions (linearly increasing on the first half of the bar corresponding to the point from zero to half of the bar length, linearly decreasing on the second half and zero elsewhere) centered on the points, that are then evaluated on a regular 1D grid.\n167:     \"\"\"\n168:     def __init__(self, samples, **kwargs):\n169:         \"\"\"\n170:         Constructor for the GaussianPerslayPhi class.\n171:   \n172:         Parameters:\n173:             samples (float numpy array): grid elements on which to evaluate the tent functions, of the form [x_1, ..., x_n].\n174:         \"\"\"\n175:         super().__init__(dynamic=True, **kwargs)\n176:         self.samples   = tf.